								["setVar:to:", "Ticks", 0]]]]]],
			"scriptComments": [[137, 23, 233, 44, false, 0, "One \"When Green Flag\" for control"],
				[234, 49, 393, 60, true, 1, "Every script that recieves this message sets up any variables or graphic elements that need to set before the project begins."],
				[719, 81, 256, 230, true, -1, "The purpose of the gameLoop is to maintain control and to avoid lag. When there are multiple forever loops in a project, it can be very difficult to handle what runs when and it can also slow down your project significantly.\r\r*Note* \rThis project only requires one forever loop for the generator, so this sprites' inclusion is superfluous, but exists for the sake of practice and consistency. In larger projects, this becomes extremely useful in avoiding lag and debugging."],
				[221.95, 210, 285, 90, true, 6, "The Start Tick prepares the variables needed for the FPS counter. They're all set to zero except fot the timerCheckpoint, which takes into account the time used for setting up the project."],
				[117.9, 344, 217, 40, false, 12, "Records every frame that passes"],
				[155, 370, 225, 44, false, 14, "Marks that one frame has passed"],
				[319, 398, 295, 41, false, 15, "Updates the FPS counter every three seconds"],
				[230, 474, 539, 39, false, 29, "When this block is called, the moments that have already passed in the timer are recorded"],
				[146, 498, 168, 39, false, 31, "Resets the frame count"],
				[74, 114, 309, 97, false, 4, "Records every frame that passes [Expand▾]\r\rThis is used to record the frames per second of the project, but for a small project such as this one, it isn't really necessary."],
				[229, 136, 422, 96, false, 5, "Every frame of the project revolves around this broadcast [Expand▾]\r\rThis message is sent every frame of the project. The reason for using this is to avoid using forever loops. Projects that depend on too many forever loops tend to lag."],
				[520, 438, 383, 82, false, 20, "Sets the FPS to the Ticks (Frames) per time passed [Expand▾]\r\rMultiplies by 100 before rounding and then multiplying to preserve two decimal places"]],
